IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`
IMPORT `wra4-ss117-119`

-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Part 5, s 120: loss of tax credits. Layer 1, part wra4.
-- The section inserts sections 36A to 36D into the Tax Credits Act 2002 (TCA) and amends TCA 38, 66 and 67.
-- The text mirrors the Social Security Fraud Act 2001 (SSFA) provisions that ss 117 to 119 encode, so the functions below
-- call those where the TCA says the same thing, and say so beside each call. Other Acts named in the text: SSAA
-- (Social Security Administration Act 1992), SSA(NI)A (its Northern Ireland counterpart).
-- Result convention: JUST FALSE / FALSE = the text says no; NOTHING = not covered (see wra4-ss96-97.l4).
-- The core Authority type has no Northern Ireland Department and no Northern Ireland Housing Executive, so the
-- bodies of 36A(2)(b) are an own type here (NEEDS-CORE.md).
-- Periods are counted with both the first and the last day included (the keel's Period).

§ `s 120 -- TCA 36A: loss of working tax credit after a benefit offence`

DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 115A of the Social Security Administration Act 1992`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 109A of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) some other penalty provision`

DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) body` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Secretary of State`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Department within the meaning of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Northern Ireland Housing Executive`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) some other body`

-- 36A(2)(a): "the appropriate penalty provision" means section 115A SSAA or section 109A SSA(NI)A. A closed definition.
-- src:2036 | (a) “the appropriate penalty provision” means section 115A of the Social Security Administration Act 1992 (penalty as alternative to prosecution) or section 109A of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992 (the corresponding provision for Northern Ireland);
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) (src:2036-2036)
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision`  @desc Which provision lets a penalty be offered as an alternative to prosecution?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the provision`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 115A of the Social Security Administration Act 1992` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 109A of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) some other penalty provision` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“the appropriate penalty provision” means section 115A ... or section 109A ..."

-- 36A(2)(b): "appropriate authority" means, for section 115A SSAA, the Secretary of State or an authority which administers
-- housing benefit or council tax benefit; for section 109A SSA(NI)A, the Department or the Northern Ireland Housing Executive.
-- src:2037 | (b) “appropriate authority” means—
-- src:2038 | (i) in relation to section 115A of the Social Security Administration Act 1992, the Secretary of State or an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit, and
-- src:2039 | (ii) in relation to section 109A of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992, the Department (within the meaning of that Act) or the Northern Ireland Housing Executive.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) (src:2037-2039)
GIVEN `the body` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) body`  @desc Which body is in question?
      `the provision` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision`  @desc Which provision for a penalty in place of prosecution is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the body is an appropriate authority in relation to the provision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the provision`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 115A of the Social Security Administration Act 1992` THEN
        CONSIDER `the body`
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Secretary of State` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Department within the meaning of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(i) in relation to section 115A ..., the Secretary of State or an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit"
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Northern Ireland Housing Executive` THEN FALSE                           -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(i) in relation to section 115A ..., the Secretary of State or an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit"
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) some other body` THEN FALSE                                                  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(i) in relation to section 115A ..., the Secretary of State or an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit"
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) section 109A of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992` THEN
        CONSIDER `the body`
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Department within the meaning of the Social Security Administration (Northern Ireland) Act 1992` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Northern Ireland Housing Executive` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the Secretary of State` THEN FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(ii) in relation to section 109A ..., the Department ... or the Northern Ireland Housing Executive"
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(ii) in relation to section 109A ..., the Department ... or the Northern Ireland Housing Executive"
        WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) some other body` THEN FALSE               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(ii) in relation to section 109A ..., the Department ... or the Northern Ireland Housing Executive"
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) some other penalty provision` THEN FALSE      -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means— (i) in relation to section 115A ... (ii) in relation to section 109A ..."

-- 36A(10): the defined terms. "benefit offence" means any of four kinds of offence committed on or after the day the Treasury
-- specifies by order; "disqualifying benefit" and "relevant offence" are defined in the Fraud Act (encoded in ss 117 and 118);
-- "disqualifying event" is the conviction, the agreement or the caution of subsection (1).
DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) kind of disqualifying event` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the conviction falling within subsection (1)(a)`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the agreement falling within subsection (1)(b)`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the caution falling within subsection (1)(c)`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) some other event`

-- src:2053 | “benefit offence“ means any of the following offences committed on or after the day specified by order made by the Treasury—
-- src:2054 | an offence in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit;
-- src:2055 | an offence in connection with the receipt or payment of any amount by way of such a benefit;
-- src:2056 | an offence committed for the purpose of facilitating the commission (whether or not by the same person) of a benefit offence;
-- src:2057 | an offence consisting in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) “benefit offence” (src:2053-2057)
GIVEN `the offence is in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the offence in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit?
      `the offence is in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the offence in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit?
      `the offence was committed for the purpose of facilitating a benefit offence` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was the offence committed for the purpose of facilitating the commission of a benefit offence, by you or by anyone else?
      `the offence consists in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does the offence consist in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence?
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE  @desc On what date was the offence committed?
      `the day specified by the Treasury by order` IS A MAYBE DATE  @desc What day has the Treasury specified by order for this purpose? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the offence is a benefit offence` MEANS
    IF `the offence is in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit`
       OR `the offence is in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit`
       OR `the offence was committed for the purpose of facilitating a benefit offence`
       OR `the offence consists in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence`
    THEN (CONSIDER `the day specified by the Treasury by order`
          WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                       -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the day is whatever the Treasury specifies by order
          WHEN JUST specified THEN
              IF `the date the offence was committed` AT LEAST specified
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE)                            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any of the following offences committed on or after the day specified by order made by the Treasury"
    ELSE JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“benefit offence” means any of the following offences" (none of the four)

-- src:2058 | “disqualifying benefit” has the meaning given in section 6A(1) of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) “disqualifying benefit” (src:2058-2058)
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit  @desc Which benefit is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` MEANS
    `s 117(2) SSFA 6A(1) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` `the benefit`   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "has the meaning given in section 6A(1) of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001", of which only paragraphs (f) and (g) are printed

-- src:2059 | “disqualifying event“ means—
-- src:2060 | the conviction falling within subsection (1)(a);
-- src:2061 | the agreement falling within subsection (1)(b);
-- src:2062 | the caution falling within subsection (1)(c);
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) “disqualifying event” (src:2059-2062)
GIVEN `the event` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) kind of disqualifying event`  @desc Which kind of event is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the event is a disqualifying event` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the event`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the conviction falling within subsection (1)(a)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the agreement falling within subsection (1)(b)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the caution falling within subsection (1)(c)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) some other event` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“disqualifying event” means— the conviction ...; the agreement ...; the caution ..."

-- src:2063 | “relevant offence” has the meaning given in section 6B of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) “relevant offence” (src:2063-2063)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the offence is a relevant offence` MEANS
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) the offence is a relevant offence` `the conviction`   -- "has the meaning given in section 6B of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001": subsection (14), encoded in s 118(5)

-- 36A(1)(b): the person, after being given a notice under subsection (2) of the appropriate penalty provision by an
-- appropriate authority, agrees in the manner the authority specifies to pay a penalty to that authority, where the
-- offence to which the notice relates is a benefit offence.
-- src:2033 | (b) after being given a notice under subsection (2) of the appropriate penalty provision by an appropriate authority, agrees in the manner specified by the appropriate authority to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision to the appropriate authority, in a case where the offence to which the notice relates is a benefit offence, or
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(1)(b) (src:2033-2033)
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision`  @desc Under which provision was a penalty offered in place of prosecution?
      `the body that gave the notice` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) body`  @desc Which body gave you the notice offering a penalty in place of prosecution?
      `the person agreed in the manner the body specified to pay the penalty to the body` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you agree, in the way that body specified, to pay it the penalty?
      `the offence to which the notice relates is a benefit offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the offence the notice relates to a benefit offence? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(1)(b) the person agreed to pay a penalty as the paragraph describes` MEANS
    IF `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` `the provision`
       AND `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(b) the body is an appropriate authority in relation to the provision` `the body that gave the notice` `the provision`
       AND `the person agreed in the manner the body specified to pay the penalty to the body`
    THEN `the offence to which the notice relates is a benefit offence`
    ELSE JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) after being given a notice under subsection (2) of the appropriate penalty provision by an appropriate authority, agrees in the manner specified by the appropriate authority to pay a penalty ... to the appropriate authority"

-- 36A(1): subsection (4) applies where the person is convicted of benefit offences (a), agrees to pay a penalty (b), or is
-- cautioned (c). The printed text of (c) is repealed by s 121(2) (see below); (c) is kept here as printed.
-- src:2031 | (1) Subsection (4) applies where a person (“the offender”)—
-- src:2032 | (a) is convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings, or
-- src:2034 | (c) is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(1) (src:2031-2034)
GIVEN `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings?
      `the offender agreed to pay a penalty as paragraph (b) describes` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Did you agree to pay a penalty in place of prosecution after being given a notice by an appropriate authority?
      `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(1) subsection (4) applies by virtue of subsection (1)` MEANS
    IF `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in any proceedings`
       OR `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences`
       OR (`the offender agreed to pay a penalty as paragraph (b) describes` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "Subsection (4) applies where a person ..."; the subsection does not say it applies only then

-- 36A(3): subsection (4) does not apply by virtue of (1)(a) if the conviction's proceedings are the current set of
-- proceedings for section 36C and the restriction in 36C(3) applies.
-- src:2040 | (3) Subsection (4) does not apply by virtue of subsection (1)(a) if, because the proceedings in which the offender was convicted constitute the current set of proceedings for the purposes of section 36C, the restriction in subsection (3) of that section applies in the offender's case.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(3) (src:2040-2040)
GIVEN `the restriction in section 36C(3) applies in the offender's case because the proceedings are the current set of proceedings for section 36C` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does the restriction in section 36C(3) apply to you because these proceedings are the current set of proceedings for section 36C?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(3) subsection (4) does not apply by virtue of subsection (1)(a)` MEANS
    IF `the restriction in section 36C(3) applies in the offender's case because the proceedings are the current set of proceedings for section 36C`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "does not apply by virtue of subsection (1)(a) if ..."; otherwise the subsection is silent

-- 36A(4): if subsection (4) applies and the offender would, apart from this section, be entitled to working tax credit at
-- any time within the disqualification period, then despite that entitlement the credit is not payable for any period
-- comprised in the disqualification period. Asked for one day: is the day within the period?
-- src:2041 | (4) If this subsection applies and the offender is a person who would, apart from this section, be entitled (whether pursuant to a single or joint claim) to working tax credit at any time within the disqualification period, then, despite that entitlement, working tax credit shall not be payable for any period comprised in the disqualification period—
-- src:2042 | (a) in the case of a single claim, to the offender, or
-- src:2043 | (b) in the case of a joint claim, to the offender or the other member of the couple.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(4) (src:2041-2043)
GIVEN `subsection (4) applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Does the penalty section apply to you (a conviction, or an agreement to pay a penalty)? Leave out if not known.
      `the offender would apart from this section be entitled to working tax credit at some time within the disqualification period` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Apart from this section, would you be entitled to working tax credit at any time within the disqualification period?
      `the claim` IS A Claimants  @desc Is the claim for working tax credit a single claim, or a joint claim?
      `the person` IS A Person  @desc Who is the person asking whether working tax credit is payable?
      `the offender` IS A Person  @desc Who is the offender?
      `the day in question` IS A DATE  @desc For which day is working tax credit in question?
      `the disqualification period` IS A Period  @desc When is the disqualification period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(4) working tax credit is not payable to the person for the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `subsection (4) applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the offender would apart from this section be entitled to working tax credit at some time within the disqualification period`
           AND `the day in question` AT LEAST `the disqualification period`'s `first day`
           AND `the day in question` AT MOST `the disqualification period`'s `last day`
        THEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(4)(a) the person is among those to whom working tax credit is not payable` `the claim` `the person` `the offender`
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "for any period comprised in the disqualification period", and only where the offender would otherwise be entitled
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "If this subsection applies"; otherwise subsection (4) is silent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether subsection (4) applies is not known

-- 36A(4)(a)-(b) (and 36C(3)(a)-(b)): to whom working tax credit is not payable: on a single claim the offender; on a joint
-- claim the offender or the other member of the couple. The persons are told apart by their reference.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(4)(a) (src:2042-2043)
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claimants  @desc Is the claim for working tax credit a single claim, or a joint claim?
      `the person` IS A Person  @desc Who is the person asking whether working tax credit is payable?
      `the offender` IS A Person  @desc Who is the offender?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(4)(a) the person is among those to whom working tax credit is not payable` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the claim`
    WHEN `a single claimant` claimant THEN
        IF `the person`'s `reference` EQUALS `the offender`'s `reference`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "(a) in the case of a single claim, to the offender"
    WHEN `joint claimants` couple THEN
        IF ((`the person`'s `reference` EQUALS (couple's `the first person`)'s `reference`) OR (`the person`'s `reference` EQUALS (couple's `the second person`)'s `reference`))
           AND ((`the offender`'s `reference` EQUALS (couple's `the first person`)'s `reference`) OR (`the offender`'s `reference` EQUALS (couple's `the second person`)'s `reference`))
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "(b) in the case of a joint claim, to the offender or the other member of the couple"

-- 36A(7): the relevant period is (a) three years in a case within (1)(a) where the benefit offence, or one of them, is a
-- relevant offence, (b) 13 weeks in a case within (1)(a) but not (a), (c) 4 weeks in a case within (1)(b) or (c).
DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) case within subsection (1)` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(a)`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(b)`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(c)`

DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) relevant period` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of three years`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 13 weeks`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 4 weeks`

-- src:2046 | (7) For the purposes of subsection (6) the relevant period is—
-- src:2047 | (a) in a case falling within subsection (1)(a) where the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence, the period of three years,
-- src:2048 | (b) in a case falling within subsection (1)(a) (but not within paragraph (a) above)), the period of 13 weeks, or
-- src:2049 | (c) in a case falling within subsection (1)(b) or (c), the period of 4 weeks.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) (src:2046-2049)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) case within subsection (1)`  @desc Is the loss of tax credit one that follows a conviction (paragraph (a) of section 36A(1)), an agreement to pay a penalty (paragraph (b)) or a caution (paragraph (c))?
      `the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit offence, or one of them, a "relevant offence"? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) relevant period`
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the relevant period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the case`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(a)` THEN
        CONSIDER `the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence`
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of three years`
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 13 weeks`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the offence is a relevant offence is not known, and the two paragraphs give different periods
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(b)` THEN JUST `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 4 weeks`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) a case within subsection (1)(c)` THEN JUST `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 4 weeks`

GIVEN `the period` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) relevant period`  @desc Which period applies to you?
      `the first day of the period` IS A DATE  @desc On what day does the period begin?
GIVETH A DATE
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the last day of the relevant period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of three years` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL (`add years` `the first day of the period` 3)) MINUS 1)
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 13 weeks` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL `the first day of the period`) PLUS 90)
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the period of 4 weeks` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL `the first day of the period`) PLUS 27)

-- 36A(6): the disqualification period, for a disqualifying event, is the relevant period beginning with a date, falling
-- after the date of the event, determined by or in accordance with regulations.
-- src:2045 | (6) For the purposes of this section, the disqualification period, in relation to any disqualifying event, means the relevant period beginning with such date, falling after the date of the disqualifying event, as may be determined by or in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36A(6) (src:2045-2045)
GIVEN `the date of the disqualifying event` IS A DATE  @desc On what date did the disqualifying event happen?
      `the date determined by or in accordance with regulations` IS A MAYBE DATE  @desc On what date do the regulations make the disqualification period begin? Leave out if not known.
      `the relevant period` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) relevant period`  @desc Which relevant period applies to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 120(2) TCA 36A(6) the disqualification period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date determined by or in accordance with regulations`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the date is whatever regulations determine
    WHEN JUST begins THEN
        IF begins GREATER THAN `the date of the disqualifying event`
        THEN JUST (Period WITH `first day` IS begins, `last day` IS `s 120(2) TCA 36A(7) the last day of the relevant period` `the relevant period` begins)
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the period begins "with such date, falling after the date of the disqualifying event"

-- 121(2): subsection (1)(c) (cautions) of TCA 36A is repealed. Asked of a caution as a ground for subsection (4).
-- src:2163 | (2) In section 36A of the Tax Credits Act 2002 (loss of tax working tax credit in case of conviction, penalty or caution for benefit offence) subsection (1)(c) (cautions) is repealed.
@ref s 121(2) TCA 36A(1)(c) (src:2163-2163)
GIVEN `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 121(2) TCA 36A(1)(c) a caution is a ground for subsection (4)` MEANS
    IF `the offender is cautioned in respect of one or more benefit offences`
    THEN JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "subsection (1)(c) (cautions) is repealed"
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the repeal removes the caution ground; it says nothing about the other grounds

-- 36A(9): "This section has effect subject to section 36B." A pointer, carried by the 36B functions below.

§ `s 120 -- TCA 36B: section 36A supplementary`

-- 36B(1): where the conviction is taken into account for 36A and is later quashed, payments and adjustments are made as if no
-- restriction had been imposed that could not have been imposed if the conviction had not taken place.
-- src:2065 | (1) Where—
-- src:2066 | (a) the conviction of any person of any offence is taken in account for the purposes of the application of section 36A in relation to that person, and
-- src:2067 | (b) that conviction is subsequently quashed,
-- src:2068 | all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under section 36A that could not have been imposed if the conviction had not taken place.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(1) (src:2065-2068)
GIVEN `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36A` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was your conviction taken into account in applying section 36A to you?
      `the conviction is subsequently quashed` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the conviction since been quashed?
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if the conviction had not taken place?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(1) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` MEANS
    IF `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36A` AND `the conviction is subsequently quashed`
    THEN (IF `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place`
          THEN JUST FALSE                                 -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed ... that could not have been imposed if the conviction had not taken place" (this one could have been)
          ELSE JUST TRUE)
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "Where (a) the conviction ... is taken in account ... and (b) that conviction is subsequently quashed"

-- 36B(2): where, after P's agreement to pay a penalty is taken into account for 36A, (a) the agreement is withdrawn under
-- subsection (5) of the appropriate penalty provision or (b) it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due,
-- adjustments are made as if no restriction had been imposed that could not have been imposed had P not agreed.
-- src:2069 | (2) Where, after the agreement of any person (“P”) to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision is taken into account for the purposes of the application of section 36A in relation to that person—
-- src:2070 | (a) P's agreement to pay the penalty is withdrawn under subsection (5) of the appropriate penalty provision, or
-- src:2071 | (b) it is decided on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1992 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998 (S.I. 1998/1506 (N.I. 10)) that the overpayment to which the agreement relates is not recoverable or due,
-- src:2072 | all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under section 36A that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(2) (src:2069-2072)
GIVEN `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account for the application of section 36A` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying section 36A to you?
      `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been withdrawn under subsection (5) of the penalty provision?
      `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has it been decided on an appeal, or under social security regulations, that the overpayment is not recoverable or due?
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if you had not agreed to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(2) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` MEANS
    IF `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account for the application of section 36A`
       AND (`the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` OR `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due`)
    THEN (IF `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty`
          THEN JUST FALSE                                 -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed ... that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty" (this one could have been)
          ELSE JUST TRUE)
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "Where, after the agreement ... is taken into account ... (a) ... or (b) ..."

-- 36B(3): where the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations after the old agreement was taken
-- into account, (a) section 36A ceases to apply by virtue of the old agreement and (b) subsection (4) applies.
-- src:2073 | (3) Where, after the agreement (“the old agreement”) of any person (“P”) to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision is taken into account for the purposes of the application of section 36A in relation to P, the amount of any overpayment made to which the penalty relates is revised on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1998 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998—
-- src:2074 | (a) section 36A shall cease to apply by virtue of the old agreement, and
-- src:2075 | (b) subsection (4) shall apply.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(3) (src:2073-2075)
GIVEN `the old agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account for the application of section 36A` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your earlier agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying section 36A to you?
      `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the amount of the overpayment been revised on an appeal, or under social security regulations?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(3) section 36A ceases to apply by virtue of the old agreement and subsection (4) applies` MEANS
    IF `the old agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account for the application of section 36A`
       AND `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "Where, after the agreement ... is taken into account ..., the amount of any overpayment ... is revised"

-- 36B(4)(a): if there is a new disqualifying event (a new agreement on the revised overpayment, or a caution for the offence),
-- the new disqualification period is reduced by the days of the old one that had expired when 36A ceased to apply. The text
-- gives no floor: the difference is returned as it stands (fork register).
DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4) what follows the revision` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(a) the new disqualification period is reduced`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(b) payments and adjustments are made as if the restriction had not been imposed`

-- src:2076 | (4) Where this subsection applies—
-- src:2077 | (a) if there is a new disqualifying event consisting of—
-- src:2078 | (i) P's agreement to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty regime in relation to the revised overpayment, or
-- src:2079 | (ii) P being cautioned in relation to the offence to which the old agreement relates,
-- src:2080 | the disqualification period relating to the new disqualifying event shall be reduced by the number of days in so much of the disqualification period relating to the old agreement as had expired when subsection 36A ceased to apply by virtue of the old agreement, and
-- src:2081 | (b) in any other case, all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under section 36A that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(4) (src:2076-2081)
GIVEN `the person agrees to pay a penalty in relation to the revised overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you agreed to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment?
      `the person is cautioned in relation to the offence to which the old agreement relates` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you been cautioned for the offence the old agreement related to?
GIVETH A `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4) what follows the revision`
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(4) the consequence of the revision` MEANS
    IF `the person agrees to pay a penalty in relation to the revised overpayment` OR `the person is cautioned in relation to the offence to which the old agreement relates`
    THEN `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(a) the new disqualification period is reduced`
    ELSE `s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(b) payments and adjustments are made as if the restriction had not been imposed`

@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(a) (src:2080-2080)
GIVEN `the number of days in the new disqualification period` IS A NUMBER  @desc How many days are in the disqualification period for the new disqualifying event?
      `the number of days of the old disqualification period that had expired` IS A NUMBER  @desc How many days of the disqualification period for the old agreement had expired when section 36A ceased to apply by virtue of the old agreement?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(4)(a) the number of days in the reduced disqualification period` MEANS
    `the number of days in the new disqualification period` MINUS `the number of days of the old disqualification period that had expired`

-- 36B(5): for 36A, (a) the date of a conviction is the date the person was found guilty (whenever sentenced), or, in the
-- absolute-discharge case of (b)(ii), the date of the order; (b) references to a conviction include a conviction with an
-- absolute or conditional discharge, an order for absolute discharge made without proceeding to a conviction, and a
-- conviction in Northern Ireland.
DECLARE `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) kind of event` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) a conviction in relation to which the court makes an order for absolute or conditional discharge`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland under section 246(3) of the Criminal Procedure (Scotland) Act 1995 without proceeding to a conviction`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) a conviction in Northern Ireland`
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) some other event`

-- src:2083 | (a) the date of a person's conviction in any proceedings of a benefit offence shall be taken to be the date on which the person was found guilty of that offence in those proceedings (whenever the person was sentenced) or in the case mentioned in paragraph (b)(ii) the date of the order for absolute discharge, and
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(a) (src:2083-2083)
GIVEN `the date on which the person was found guilty` IS A DATE  @desc On what date were you found guilty of the offence (whenever you were sentenced)?
      `the case is an order for absolute discharge made without proceeding to a conviction` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was your case dealt with by an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland without proceeding to a conviction?
      `the date of the order for absolute discharge` IS A DATE  @desc On what date was the order for absolute discharge made?
GIVETH A DATE
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(a) the date of the conviction` MEANS
    IF `the case is an order for absolute discharge made without proceeding to a conviction`
    THEN `the date of the order for absolute discharge`
    ELSE `the date on which the person was found guilty`

-- src:2084 | (b) references to a conviction include references to—
-- src:2085 | (i) a conviction in relation to which the court makes an order for absolute or conditional discharge,
-- src:2086 | (ii) an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland under section 246(3) of the Criminal Procedure (Scotland) Act 1995 without proceeding to a conviction, and
-- src:2087 | (iii) a conviction in Northern Ireland.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) (src:2084-2087)
GIVEN `the event` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) kind of event`  @desc Which kind of event is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) the event counts as a conviction for section 36A` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the event`
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) a conviction in relation to which the court makes an order for absolute or conditional discharge` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland under section 246(3) of the Criminal Procedure (Scotland) Act 1995 without proceeding to a conviction` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) a conviction in Northern Ireland` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) some other event` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "references to a conviction include references to" three cases; "include" does not close the list

-- 36B(6): "the appropriate penalty provision" has the meaning given by 36A(2)(a).
-- src:2088 | (6) In this section “the appropriate penalty provision” has the meaning given by section 36A(2)(a).
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36B(6) (src:2088-2088)
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision`  @desc Which provision lets a penalty be offered as an alternative to prosecution?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36B(6) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` `the provision`

§ `s 120 -- TCA 36C: loss of working tax credit for repeated benefit fraud`

-- 36C(1): the five conditions and the restriction. The five-year count, the earlier disqualifying event and the date lists
-- are those of SSFA 7(1)(b) (s 119(5)); 36C says the same in the same words.
-- src:2090 | (1) If—
-- src:2091 | (a) a person (“the offender”) is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings (“the current set of proceedings”),
-- src:2092 | (b) within the period of five years ending on the date on which the benefit offence was, or any of them were, committed, one or more disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender (the event, or the most recent of them, being referred to in this section as “the earlier disqualifying event”),
-- src:2093 | (c) the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for the purposes of any previous application of this section in relation to the offender,
-- src:2094 | (d) the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account as an earlier disqualifying event for the purposes of any previous application of this section in relation to the offender, and
-- src:2095 | (e) the offender is a person who would, apart from this section, be entitled (whether pursuant to a single or joint claim) to working tax credit at any time within the disqualification period,
-- src:2096 | then, despite that entitlement, the restriction in subsection (3) shall apply in relation to the payment of that benefit in the offender's case.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(1) (src:2090-2096)
GIVEN `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings?
      `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each disqualifying event happen in relation to you?
      `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has this set of proceedings been left out of every earlier application of this section to you?
      `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the earlier disqualifying event been left out of every earlier application of this section to you?
      `the offender would apart from this section be entitled to working tax credit at some time within the disqualification period` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Apart from this section, would you be entitled to working tax credit at any time within the disqualification period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(1) the restriction in subsection (3) applies` MEANS
    IF `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings`
       AND `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) a disqualifying event occurred within five years before a benefit offence` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender`
       AND `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section`
       AND `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section`
       AND `the offender would apart from this section be entitled to working tax credit at some time within the disqualification period`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "If (a) ..., (b) ..., (c) ..., (d) ..., and (e) ..., then ... the restriction in subsection (3) shall apply"

-- 36C(2): the restriction does not apply if the benefit offence, or any of them, is a relevant offence.
-- src:2097 | (2) The restriction in subsection (3) does not apply if the benefit offence referred to in subsection (1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(2) (src:2097-2097)
GIVEN `the benefit offence referred to in subsection (1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit offence, or any of them, a "relevant offence"? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(2) the restriction in subsection (3) does not apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the benefit offence referred to in subsection (1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "does not apply if ... a relevant offence"; the subsection does not say it applies otherwise
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the offence is a relevant offence is not known

-- 36C(3): working tax credit is not payable for any period within the disqualification period: single claim, the offender;
-- joint claim, the offender or the other member of the couple.
-- src:2098 | (3) Working tax credit shall not be payable for any period comprised in the disqualification period—
-- src:2099 | (a) in the case of a single claim, to the offender, or
-- src:2100 | (b) in the case of a joint claim, to the offender or the other member of the couple.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(3) (src:2098-2100)
GIVEN `the restriction in subsection (3) applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Does the restriction for repeated benefit fraud apply to you? Leave out if not known.
      `the claim` IS A Claimants  @desc Is the claim for working tax credit a single claim, or a joint claim?
      `the person` IS A Person  @desc Who is the person asking whether working tax credit is payable?
      `the offender` IS A Person  @desc Who is the offender?
      `the day in question` IS A DATE  @desc For which day is working tax credit in question?
      `the disqualification period` IS A Period  @desc When is the disqualification period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(3) working tax credit is not payable to the person for the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the restriction in subsection (3) applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the day in question` AT LEAST `the disqualification period`'s `first day`
           AND `the day in question` AT MOST `the disqualification period`'s `last day`
        THEN `s 120(2) TCA 36A(4)(a) the person is among those to whom working tax credit is not payable` `the claim` `the person` `the offender`
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "for any period comprised in the disqualification period"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the restriction is in subsection (3) only where it applies
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the restriction applies is not known

-- 36C(5): the disqualification period, in an offender's case, is the relevant period beginning with a prescribed date
-- falling after the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings. 36C(6): the relevant period is three years
-- if, within five years ending on the earlier disqualifying event, a previous one occurred; otherwise 26 weeks. Both are
-- the Fraud Act's 7(6) and 7(6A) over again, and are answered by them.
-- src:2102 | (5) For the purposes of this section the disqualification period, in an offender's case, means the relevant period beginning with a prescribed date falling after the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(5) (src:2102-2102)
GIVEN `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings` IS A DATE  @desc On what date were you convicted in the current set of proceedings?
      `the prescribed date` IS A MAYBE DATE  @desc What is the prescribed date on which the disqualification period begins? Leave out if not known.
      `the relevant period` IS A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period`  @desc Which relevant period applies to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(5) the disqualification period` MEANS
    `s 119(6) SSFA 7(6) the disqualification period` `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings` `the prescribed date` `the relevant period`

-- src:2103 | (6) For the purposes of subsection (5) the relevant period is—
-- src:2104 | (a) in a case where, within the period of five years ending on the date on which the earlier disqualifying event occurred, a previous disqualifying event occurred in relation to the offender, the period of three years;
-- src:2105 | (b) in any other case, 26 weeks.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(6) (src:2103-2105)
GIVEN `the dates on which the earlier disqualifying event is taken to have occurred` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did the earlier disqualifying event happen (for a set of proceedings with several convictions, the date of each conviction)?
      `the dates on which a previous disqualifying event is taken to have occurred` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did a previous disqualifying event happen (for a set of proceedings with several convictions, the date of each conviction)?
GIVETH A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period`
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(6) the relevant period` MEANS
    `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the relevant period` `the dates on which the earlier disqualifying event is taken to have occurred` `the dates on which a previous disqualifying event is taken to have occurred`

-- 36C(7): the defined terms. Each of "benefit offence", "disqualifying benefit", "disqualifying event" and "relevant offence"
-- is the meaning given in 36A(10) or the Fraud Act, and is answered by the function that encodes that meaning.
-- src:2107 | “appropriate penalty provision” has the meaning given in section 36A(2)(a);
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) “appropriate penalty provision” (src:2107-2107)
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) penalty provision`  @desc Which provision lets a penalty be offered as an alternative to prosecution?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(2)(a) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` `the provision`

-- src:2108 | “benefit offence“ means any of the following offences committed on or after the day specified by order made by the Treasury—
-- src:2109 | an offence in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit;
-- src:2110 | an offence in connection with the receipt or payment of any amount by way of such a benefit;
-- src:2111 | an offence committed for the purpose of facilitating the commission (whether or not by the same person) of a benefit offence;
-- src:2112 | an offence consisting in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) “benefit offence” (src:2108-2112)
GIVEN `the offence is in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the offence in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit?
      `the offence is in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the offence in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit?
      `the offence was committed for the purpose of facilitating a benefit offence` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was the offence committed for the purpose of facilitating the commission of a benefit offence, by you or by anyone else?
      `the offence consists in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does the offence consist in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence?
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE  @desc On what date was the offence committed?
      `the day specified by the Treasury by order` IS A MAYBE DATE  @desc What day has the Treasury specified by order for this purpose? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) the offence is a benefit offence` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the offence is a benefit offence` `the offence is in connection with a claim for a disqualifying benefit` `the offence is in connection with the receipt or payment of an amount by way of a disqualifying benefit` `the offence was committed for the purpose of facilitating a benefit offence` `the offence consists in an attempt or conspiracy to commit a benefit offence` `the date the offence was committed` `the day specified by the Treasury by order`

-- src:2113 | “disqualifying benefit” has the meaning given in section 6A(1) of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) “disqualifying benefit” (src:2113-2113)
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit  @desc Which benefit is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` MEANS
    `s 117(2) SSFA 6A(1) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` `the benefit`   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "has the meaning given in section 6A(1) of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001"

-- src:2114 | “disqualifying event“ has the meaning given in section 36A(10);
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) “disqualifying event” (src:2114-2114)
GIVEN `the event` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) kind of disqualifying event`  @desc Which kind of event is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) the event is a disqualifying event` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36A(10) the event is a disqualifying event` `the event`

-- src:2115 | “relevant offence” has the meaning given in section 6B of the Social Security Fraud Act 2001.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) “relevant offence” (src:2115-2115)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(7) the offence is a relevant offence` MEANS
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) the offence is a relevant offence` `the conviction`

-- 36C(8): one disqualifying event for a set of proceedings with several convictions; (a) and (b) are the Fraud Act's 7(8A)(a)
-- and (b); (c) and (d) are carried by the list inputs of 36C(6).
-- src:2116 | (8) Where a person is convicted of more than one benefit offence in the same set of proceedings, there is to be only one disqualifying event in respect of that set of proceedings for the purposes of this section and—
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(8) (src:2116-2116)
GIVEN `the number of benefit offences of which the person is convicted in the set of proceedings` IS A NUMBER  @desc Of how many benefit offences are you convicted in this set of proceedings?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(8) the number of disqualifying events in the set of proceedings` MEANS
    `s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A) the number of disqualifying events in the set of proceedings` `the number of benefit offences of which the person is convicted in the set of proceedings`

-- src:2117 | (a) subsection (1)(b) is satisfied if any of the convictions take place in the five year period there;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(8)(a) (src:2117-2117)
GIVEN `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which the convictions in the earlier set of proceedings took place` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each conviction in the earlier set of proceedings take place?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(8)(a) subsection (1)(b) is satisfied` MEANS
    `s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(a) subsection (1)(b) is satisfied` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which the convictions in the earlier set of proceedings took place`

-- src:2118 | (b) the event is taken into account for the purposes of subsection (1)(d) if any of the convictions have been taken into account as mentioned there;
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36C(8)(b) (src:2118-2118)
GIVEN `whether each of the convictions has been taken into account for a previous application` IS A LIST OF BOOLEAN  @desc For each conviction in the earlier set of proceedings: has it been taken into account for an earlier application of this section?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36C(8)(b) the event is taken into account for the purposes of subsection (1)(d)` MEANS
    `s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(b) the event is taken into account for the purposes of subsection (1)(d)` `whether each of the convictions has been taken into account for a previous application`

§ `s 120 -- TCA 36D: section 36C supplementary`

-- 36D(1): a quashed conviction taken into account for 36C: same as 36B(1).
-- src:2125 | (1) Where—
-- src:2126 | (a) the conviction of any person of any offence is taken into account for the purposes of the application of section 36C in relation to that person, and
-- src:2127 | (b) that conviction is subsequently quashed,
-- src:2128 | all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under section 36C that could not have been imposed if the conviction had not taken place.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(1) (src:2125-2128)
GIVEN `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36C` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was your conviction taken into account in applying section 36C to you?
      `the conviction is subsequently quashed` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the conviction since been quashed?
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if the conviction had not taken place?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(1) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(1) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` `the conviction is taken into account for the application of section 36C` `the conviction is subsequently quashed` `the restriction could have been imposed even if the conviction had not taken place`

-- 36D(2), (3): the same as SSFA 7(7A) and 7(7B), for 36C.
-- src:2129 | (2) Subsection (3) applies where, after the agreement of any person (“P”) to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision is taken into account for the purposes of the application of section 36C in relation to that person—
-- src:2130 | (a) P's agreement to pay the penalty is withdrawn under subsection (5) of the appropriate penalty provision,
-- src:2131 | (b) it is decided on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1998 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998 (S.I. 1998/1506 (N.I. 10)) that any overpayment made to which the agreement relates is not recoverable or due, or
-- src:2132 | (c) the amount of any over payment to which the penalty relates is revised on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1998 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998 and there is no new agreement by P to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision in relation to the revised overpayment.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(2) (src:2129-2132)
GIVEN `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying section 36C to the person` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying section 36C to you?
      `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been withdrawn under subsection (5) of the penalty provision?
      `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has it been decided on an appeal, or under social security regulations, that the overpayment is not recoverable or due?
      `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the amount of the overpayment been revised on an appeal, or under social security regulations?
      `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you agreed a new penalty on the revised overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(2) subsection (3) applies` MEANS
    `s 119(8) SSFA 7(7A) subsection (7B) applies` `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying section 36C to the person` `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment`

-- src:2133 | (3) In those circumstances, all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under section 36C that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(3) (src:2133-2133)
GIVEN `subsection (3) applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do the circumstances in section 36D(2) exist for you? Leave out if not known.
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if you had not agreed to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(3) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` MEANS
    `s 119(8) SSFA 7(7B) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` `subsection (3) applies` `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty`

-- 36D(4): the date and meaning of a conviction for 36C: the same as 36B(5).
-- src:2135 | (a) the date of a person's conviction in any proceedings of a benefit offence shall be taken to be the date on which the person was found guilty of that offence in those proceedings (whenever the person was sentenced) or in the case mentioned in paragraph (b)(ii) the date of the order for absolute discharge, and
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(4)(a) (src:2135-2135)
GIVEN `the date on which the person was found guilty` IS A DATE  @desc On what date were you found guilty of the offence (whenever you were sentenced)?
      `the case is an order for absolute discharge made without proceeding to a conviction` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Was your case dealt with by an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland without proceeding to a conviction?
      `the date of the order for absolute discharge` IS A DATE  @desc On what date was the order for absolute discharge made?
GIVETH A DATE
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(4)(a) the date of the conviction` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(a) the date of the conviction` `the date on which the person was found guilty` `the case is an order for absolute discharge made without proceeding to a conviction` `the date of the order for absolute discharge`

-- src:2136 | (b) references to a conviction include references to—
-- src:2137 | (i) a conviction in relation to which the court makes an order for absolute or conditional discharge,
-- src:2138 | (ii) an order for absolute discharge made by a court of summary jurisdiction in Scotland under section 246(3) of the Criminal Procedure (Scotland) Act 1995 without proceeding to a conviction, and
-- src:2139 | (iii) a conviction in Northern Ireland.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(4)(b) (src:2136-2139)
GIVEN `the event` IS A `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) kind of event`  @desc Which kind of event is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(4)(b) the event counts as a conviction for section 36C` MEANS
    `s 120(2) TCA 36B(5)(b) the event counts as a conviction for section 36A` `the event`

-- 36D(5): in 36C, references to any previous application of that section (a) include a previous application of a Northern
-- Ireland provision corresponding to it, but (b) do not include a previous application whose effect was to impose a
-- restriction for a period within the same disqualification period.
-- src:2140 | (5) In section 36C references to any previous application of that section—
-- src:2141 | (a) include references to any previous application of a provision having an effect in Northern Ireland corresponding to provision made by that section, but
-- src:2142 | (b) do not include references to any previous application of that section the effect of which was to impose a restriction for a period comprised in the same disqualification period.
@ref s 120(2) TCA 36D(5) (src:2140-2142)
GIVEN `it is a previous application of section 36C` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is it an earlier application of section 36C to you?
      `it is a previous application of a corresponding provision having effect in Northern Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is it an earlier application to you of the corresponding Northern Ireland provision?
      `its effect was to impose a restriction for a period comprised in the same disqualification period` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did it impose a restriction for a period within the same disqualification period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(2) TCA 36D(5) the application counts as a previous application of section 36C` MEANS
    IF `its effect was to impose a restriction for a period comprised in the same disqualification period`
    THEN JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) do not include references to any previous application of that section the effect of which was to impose a restriction for a period comprised in the same disqualification period"
    ELSE (IF `it is a previous application of section 36C` OR `it is a previous application of a corresponding provision having effect in Northern Ireland`
          THEN JUST TRUE
          ELSE NOTHING)                                   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "references to any previous application of that section (a) include ... (b) do not include ..."; the two paragraphs do not say what else counts

§ `s 120 -- TCA 38, 66, 67`

-- TCA 38(1)(ca): a decision under section 36A or 36C that working tax credit is not payable (or is not payable for a
-- particular period) can be appealed. The other decisions in 38(1) are not printed.
DECLARE `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) kind of decision` IS ONE OF
    `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) a decision under section 36A that working tax credit is not payable, or not payable for a particular period`
    `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) a decision under section 36C that working tax credit is not payable, or not payable for a particular period`
    `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) some other decision`

-- src:2145 | (b) after that paragraph there is inserted—
-- src:2146 | (ca) a decision under section 36A or 36C that working tax credit is not payable (or is not payable for a particular period), and
@ref s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) (src:2145-2146)
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) kind of decision`  @desc What kind of decision are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) an appeal lies against the decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) a decision under section 36A that working tax credit is not payable, or not payable for a particular period` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) a decision under section 36C that working tax credit is not payable, or not payable for a particular period` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 120(3)(b) TCA 38(1)(ca) some other decision` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): paragraph (ca) is added to a list whose other paragraphs are not printed

-- TCA 67: "cautioned", in relation to any person and any offence, means cautioned after the person concerned has admitted the offence.
-- src:2156 | “cautioned”, in relation to any person and any offence, means cautioned after the person concerned has admitted the offence; and “caution” is to be interpreted accordingly;
@ref s 120(5) TCA 67 “cautioned” (src:2156-2156)
GIVEN `the person was cautioned` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Were you cautioned for the offence?
      `the person admitted the offence before being cautioned` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Had you admitted the offence before you were cautioned?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 120(5) TCA 67 the person is cautioned in relation to the offence` MEANS
    IF `the person was cautioned` AND `the person admitted the offence before being cautioned`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE FALSE                                            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“cautioned” ... means cautioned after the person concerned has admitted the offence"
